+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+-   **Coordinate System:** The source code assumes input positions are in $[-1, 1]$ range for texture sampling. If positions are in a different range, the texture coordinate calculation must be adjusted [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Blending:** The technique relies on alpha blending. The source mentions using `GL_MAX` blending rather than `GL_FUNC_ADD` for certain effects, but the default blending equation is `GL_FUNC_ADD` [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Velocity Magnitude:** The length of the line is directly proportional to the velocity vector's magnitude. Large velocities may cause lines to extend far outside the voxel, potentially causing visual clutter or occlusion issues.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with the number of grid cells, as each cell generates one line strip with two vertices [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low, requiring only a VBO for points and a 3D texture for velocities [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, efficient for structured grids, and visually intuitive for flow fields [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires `GL_MAX` blending for correct visual results and depends on proper velocity magnitude scaling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Visualizing structured velocity fields such as fluid simulation grids [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Unstructured particle systems (use [GPU Particle Rendering via Geometry Shader](4444-gpu-particle-rendering-via-geometry-shader.md)) or when additive blending is desired [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of grid cells. Each cell generates a constant number of vertices (2) and fragments.
+-   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the VBO and $O(V)$ for the volume texture, where $V$ is the volume resolution.
+-   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; efficient on GPU; provides clear vector direction and magnitude visualization.
+-   **Limitations:** Sparse representation; may not show flow continuity as well as streamlines; sensitive to velocity magnitude scaling.
 
 ## Background
-This technique was described by Philip Rideout in a blog post on November 28th, 2010, as part of the "Volumetric Splatting" series [S1, unofficial]. It is presented as a simple and "beautiful" way to visualize velocity grids using modern OpenGL geometry shaders [S1, unofficial].
